event: response.function_call_arguments.done
data: {"type":"response.function_call_arguments.done","sequence_number":1584,"item_id":"fc_0d9ad8b970f7f85d016adb1a5183b88191b2e4e2055e29f2cc","output_index":3,"arguments":"{\"artifact_json\":\"{\\\"status\\\":\\\"PROOF_CANDIDATE\\\",\\\"target\\\":\\\"For every nonnegative integer n and every nonempty compact E ⊆ R^n, with I=[0,1] and E×I given the metric induced by the identification (x,t)↦(x,t) in Euclidean R^(n+1), dim_H(E×I)=dim_H(E)+1. Hausdorff dimension uses the pinned convention dim_H(∅)=0.\\\",\\\"proof\\\":\\\"Write d=dim_H(E). All metrics below are Euclidean; the product metric is exactly the Euclidean metric under (x,t)↦(x,t)∈R^(n+1). We prove the two inequalities.\\\\n\\\\nUpper bound. Fix any real q>d. Then H^q(E)=0. Given η>0 and a sufficiently small scale δ≤1, choose a countable cover (U_j) of E with 0<r_j:=diam(U_j)≤δ and Σ_j r_j^q<η; one may use positive-radius balls in the Hausdorff cover, so zero-diameter covering sets need not be used. Partition I into at most 2/r_j intervals of length at most r_j (since r_j≤1). The corresponding at most 2/r_j sets U_j×J cover U_j×I; each has Euclidean diameter at most √2 r_j. Thus the sum of their (q+1)-powers of diameters is at most 2(√2)^(q+1) r_j^q. Taking all j gives a cover of E×I of arbitrarily small mesh whose sum of (q+1)-powers is at most 2(√2)^(q+1)η. Since η is arbitrary, H^(q+1)(E×I)=0, hence dim_H(E×I)≤q+1. Letting q decrease to d gives dim_H(E×I)≤d+1.\\\\n\\\\nLower bound. For every real s with 0≤s<d, apply Frostman's lemma to the nonempty compact set E in R^n to obtain a nonzero finite Borel measure μ supported on E and a constant C such that μ(B(x,r))≤C r^s for every x∈R^n and r>0. Let λ be Lebesgue measure restricted to I and let ν=μ×λ, a nonzero finite Borel measure supported on E×I. For any z=(x,t)∈R^(n+1) and r>0, the Euclidean ball B(z,r) is contained in B(x,r)×(t−r,t+r); consequently ν(B(z,r))≤2C r^(s+1). By the mass distribution principle, dim_H(E×I)≥s+1. Taking the supremum over 0≤s<d yields dim_H(E×I)≥d+1 when d>0. If d=0, choose x₀∈E (possible by nonemptiness) and instead take μ to be the unit point mass at x₀. Then ν=δ_(x₀)×λ is nonzero and ν(B(z,r))≤2r for all z,r, so the same mass distribution principle gives dim_H(E×I)≥1=d+1. Combining both bounds proves the equality.\\\\n\\\\nThe correspondence of the displayed product to Euclidean R^(n+1) is isometric: the squared distance between (x,t) and (y,u) is ||x−y||₂²+|t−u|² on both sides, so no change-of-metric dimension result is needed.\\\",\\\"cited_results\\\":[{\\\"name\\\":\\\"Frostman's lemma (Hausdorff-dimension form)\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"standard external theorem; source registration/provenance still required\\\",\\\"statement_used\\\":\\\"If K is a nonempty compact subset of Euclidean space and 0≤s<dim_H(K), there is a nonzero finite Borel measure supported on K with μ(B(x,r))≤C r^s for every center x and radius r>0.\\\",\\\"applicability_obligations\\\":[\\\"E is compact and nonempty by the frozen target.\\\",\\\"The ambient space is Euclidean R^n; the measure theorem's ball convention and range of s must match the stated bound.\\\",\\\"This application is used only for s<dim_H(E); the zero-dimensional case is handled separately by a point mass.\\\",\\\"The exact primary source/version and complete theorem context have not been checked. This is a source-completion obligation, not by itself a mathematical gap.\\\"]},{\\\"name\\\":\\\"Mass distribution principle\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"standard external theorem; source registration/provenance still required\\\",\\\"statement_used\\\":\\\"If a nonzero finite Borel measure supported on A satisfies μ(B(x,r))≤C r^t uniformly in x and r>0, then dim_H(A)≥t.\\\",\\\"applicability_obligations\\\":[\\\"ν=μ×Lebesgue|_[0,1] is a nonzero finite Borel measure and is supported on E×I.\\\",\\\"The displayed Euclidean ball containment implies ν(B(z,r))≤2C r^(s+1); for the point-mass case the same bound holds with exponent 1.\\\",\\\"The exact cited formulation must allow the ambient Euclidean metric and the stated support/ball conventions; exact source/version remains to be registered and checked.\\\"]},{\\\"name\\\":\\\"Mathlib Hausdorff-dimension source candidate: Mathlib/Topology/MetricSpace/HausdorffDimension.lean\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"candidate module only; no declaration verified in this stage\\\",\\\"statement_used\\\":\\\"The frozen source-context identifies this module as proving dimH_empty=0, but that result is not used in this proof.\\\",\\\"applicability_obligations\\\":[\\\"A pinned-source probe and full declaration/signature inspection are needed before treating any declaration there as an M input.\\\",\\\"The exact frozen target already fixes the empty-set convention; E is nonempty, so no empty-set case is used.\\\"]}],\\\"boundary_cases\\\":[\\\"n=0: R^0 consists of one point, and nonempty E is that point. The point-mass lower-bound argument gives dimension at least 1; the upper-bound covering argument gives at most 1.\\\",\\\"dim_H(E)=0: use a point mass at an arbitrary point of E rather than Frostman's lemma with a negative or limiting exponent; the product with interval Lebesgue measure gives the lower bound 1.\\\",\\\"dim_H(E)>0: apply the Frostman argument for every 0≤s<dim_H(E), then take the supremum.\\\",\\\"E=∅ is excluded by the frozen hypothesis; under the stated convention the claimed formula would not hold for E=∅.\\\",\\\"The product metric is not left implicit: concatenation into R^(n+1) preserves distances exactly.\\\"],\\\"unresolved_points\\\":[\\\"The mathlib search/read tools returned a phase-ended PermissionError, so no pinned declarations or full signatures could be inspected. No mathlib theorem is claimed or used; the only module candidate recorded is supplied by source-context.md.\\\",\\\"The external source records for Frostman's lemma and the mass distribution principle, including exact primary-source identity, theorem locations, edition/version, full context and source eligibility, still need completion in later source review.\\\",\\\"This is a natural-language proof candidate, not an NL0 graph, Audit A, or Lean acceptance result.\\\"]}\"}"}

