+1). By the mass distribution principle, dim_H(E×I)≥s+1. Taking the supremum over 0≤s<d yields dim_H(E×I)≥d+1 when d>0. If d=0, choose x₀∈E (possible by nonemptiness) and instead take μ to be the unit point mass at x₀. Then ν=δ_(x₀)×λ is nonzero and ν(B(z,r))≤2r for all z,r, so the same mass distribution principle gives dim_H(E×I)≥1=d+1. Combining both bounds proves the equality.\\\\n\\\\nThe correspondence of the displayed product to Euclidean R^(n+1) is isometric: the squared distance between (x,t) and (y,u) is ||x−y||₂²+|t−u|² on both sides, so no change-of-metric dimension result is needed.\\\",\\\"cited_results\\\":[{\\\"name\\\":\\\"Frostman's lemma (Hausdorff-dimension form)\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"standard external theorem; source registration/provenance still required\\\",\\\"statement_used\\\":\\\"If K is a nonempty compact subset of Euclidean space and 0≤s<dim_H(K), there is a nonzero finite Borel measure supported on K with μ(B(x,r))≤C r^s for every center x and radius r>0.\\\",\\\"applicability_obligations\\\":[\\\"E is compact and nonempty by the frozen target.\\\",\\\"The ambient space is Euclidean R^n; the measure theorem's ball convention and range of s must match the stated bound.\\\",\\\"This application is used only for s<dim_H(E); the zero-dimensional case is handled separately by a point mass.\\\",\\\"The exact primary source/version and complete theorem context have not been checked. This is a source-completion obligation, not by itself a mathematical gap.\\\"]},{\\\"name\\\":\\\"Mass distribution principle\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"standard external theorem; source registration/provenance still required\\\",\\\"statement_used\\\":\\\"If a nonzero finite Borel measure supported on A satisfies μ(B(x,r))≤C r^t uniformly in x and r>0, then dim_H(A)≥t.\\\",\\\"applicability_obligations\\\":[\\\"ν=μ×Lebesgue|_[0,1] is a nonzero finite Borel measure and is supported on E×I.\\\",\\\"The displayed Euclidean ball containment implies ν(B(z,r))≤2C r^(s+1); for the point-mass case the same bound holds with exponent 1.\\\",\\\"The exact cited formulation must allow the ambient Euclidean metric and the stated support/ball conventions; exact source/version remains to be registered and checked.\\\"]},{\\\"name\\\":\\\"Mathlib Hausdorff-dimension source candidate: Mathlib/Topology/MetricSpace/HausdorffDimension.lean\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"candidate module only; no declaration verified in this stage\\\",\\\"statement_used\\\":\\\"The frozen source-context identifies this module as proving dimH_empty=0, but that result is not used in this proof.\\\",\\\"applicability_obligations\\\":[\\\"A pinned-source probe and full declaration/signature inspection are needed before treating any declaration there as an M input.\\\",\\\"The exact frozen target already fixes the empty-set convention; E is nonempty, so no empty-set case is used.\\\"]}],\\\"boundary_cases\\\":[\\\"n=0: R^0 consists of one point, and nonempty E is that point. The point-mass lower-bound argument gives dimension at least 1; the upper-bound covering argument gives at most 1.\\\",\\\"dim_H(E)=0: use a point mass at an arbitrary point of E rather than Frostman's lemma with a negative or limiting exponent; the product with interval Lebesgue measure gives the lower bound 1.\\\",\\\"dim_H(E)>0: apply the Frostman argument for every 0≤s<dim_H(E), then take the supremum.\\\",\\\"E=∅ is excluded by the frozen hypothesis; under the stated convention the claimed formula would not hold for E=∅.\\\",\\\"The product metric is not left implicit: concatenation into R^(n+1) preserves distances exactly.\\\"],\\\"unresolved_points\\\":[\\\"The mathlib search/read tools returned a phase-ended PermissionError, so no pinned declarations or full signatures could be inspected. No mathlib theorem is claimed or used; the only module candidate recorded is supplied by source-context.md.\\\",\\\"The external source records for Frostman's lemma and the mass distribution principle, including exact pri